<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christoph Bernhard von Galen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a>–<a href="/years/1678">1678</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christoph Bernhard von Galen was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster from 1650 to 1678. He broke the independence of the city of Münster in 1661 and built a strong standing army, earning the nickname 'Bomben-Bernd' for his use of artillery. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in 1665–1666 and again from 1672 as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christoph-bernhard-von-galen.jpg" alt="Christoph Bernhard von Galen" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Heimbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernard_von_Galen_on_Horse_by_Wolfgang_Heimbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christoph Bernhard von Galen was born on 12 October 1606 to Lutheran parents of the noble Westphalian von Galen family. His father, Dietrich von Galen, held estates in the Baltic region and the title of Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>. On 15 February 1607, during a state assembly in Münster, Dietrich killed the hereditary marshal of Münster, Gerd Morrien zu Nordkirchen, and spent twelve years in detention at Bevergern Castle, where his wife joined him of her own accord.</p><p>In 1616 the boy was placed with his uncle Heinrich von Galen, a canon of Münster, who had him educated as a Catholic by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> at the Paulinum. He began working for the cathedral chapter at 13, completed his Jesuit schooling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, and returned to Münster in July 1627. He became treasurer of the cathedral in 1630 and a spiritual adviser in 1634, and was sent on many diplomatic missions, among them negotiations with the imperial generals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Left poor by the loss of his father's inheritance, he took holy orders, yet fought for Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a> in the last stages of the war. In 1650 he succeeded Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> as prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. Ruling the largest ecclesiastical principality of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> with a strong army, he became an important figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1664 he was chosen one of the directors of the imperial army raised against the Turks, though his troops arrived too late to fight. His war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> on the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a> ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</a>, forced a disadvantageous peace at Cleve in 1666.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a> his troops, helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, took Groenlo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a> and Coevorden, but stalled before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> when deliberate flooding made the fields and marshes almost impassable. In October 1674 he withdrew and gave up his attempt to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in the eastern provinces. In 1675 he changed sides to fight for Emperor Leopold I and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, conquered the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI of Sweden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Galen died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaus">Ahaus</a> on 19 September 1678. He sought to reform the church, but his main energies went into his own power and prestige, and he rid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> of the foreign armies that had occupied it since 1648. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> he is remembered as Bommen Berend, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> still celebrates the breaking of his siege every 28 August.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christoph Bernhard von Galen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Christoph Bernhard von Galen</b> waged wars against the Dutch Republic, and in the 18th century the minister Franz von Fürstenberg reformed schools and founded the university.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-munster">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rinkerode, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaus">Ahaus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.85,7.68&amp;year=1652" aria-label="Rinkerode on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4723 371 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christoph Bernhard von Galen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5173 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5173 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.85,7.68&amp;year=1652">What was here in 1652? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
